          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            %a{:href => "#Ravenel92"}> Ravenel 1992, p. 24
            \:
          %blockquote
            %p
              We use the word ‘chromatic’ here for the following reason. The
              = succeed "-th" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              = succeed "-periodic" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi v
                        %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v_n
          %p
            By the construction of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+oriented+cohomology+theories"} complex oriented cohomology theories
            from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+groups"} formal groups
            (via the
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Landweber+exact+functor+theorem"} Landweber exact functor theorem
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/height+of+a+formal+group"} height filtration
            of formal groups induces a “chromatic”
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/filtration"} filtration
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+oriented+cohomology+theories"} complex oriented cohomology theories
            %em Chromatic homotopy theory
            is the study of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+homotopy+theory"} stable homotopy theory
            and specifically of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+oriented+cohomology+theories"} complex oriented cohomology theories
            by means of and along this
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chromatic+filtration"} chromatic filtration
          %p
            More abstractly, this filtering is induced by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prime+spectrum+of+a+symmetric+monoidal+stable+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} prime spectrum of a symmetric monoidal stable (∞,1)-category
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category+of+spectra"} (∞,1)-category of spectra
            for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/p-localization"} p-local
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+spectra"} finite spectra
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Morava+K-theories"} Morava K-theories
          %p
            More in detail, for each
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prime"} prime
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p \in \mathbb{N}
            and for each
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \in \mathbb{N}
            there is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bousfield+localization+of+spectra"} Bousfield localization of spectra
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mi n
                  %mo ≔
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mrow
                      %mi K
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∨
                      %mi ⋯
                      %mo ∨
                      %mi K
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  L_n \coloneqq L_{K(0)\vee \cdots \vee K(n)}
                  \\,,
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K(n)
            is the
            = succeed "th" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Morava+K-theory"} Morava K-theory
            (for the given
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prime"} prime
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chromatic+tower"} chromatic tower
            which for each spectrum
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is of the form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mi n
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mn 0
                  %mi X
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  X \to \cdots \to L_n X \to L_{n-1} X \to \cdots \to L_0 X
                  \\,.
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chromatic+convergence+theorem"} chromatic convergence theorem
            states mild conditions under which the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+limit"} homotopy limit
            over this tower is the
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bousfield+localization+of+spectra"} localization
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi p
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  X \to X_{(p)}
          %p
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            Since moreover
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mi n
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_n X
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+fiber+product"} homotopy fiber product
            (see at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smash+product+theorem"} smash product theorem
            and see
            %a{:href => "fracture+theorem#ExampleOfChromaticFracturing"} this remark
            at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fracture+square"} fracture square
            )
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mi n
                  %mi X
                  %mo ≃
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mrow
                      %mi K
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi X
                  %munder
                    %mo ×
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi L
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                          %mo −
                          %mn 1
                      %msub
                        %mi L
                        %mrow
                          %mi K
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi n
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi X
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  L_n X \simeq L_{K(n)}X \underset{L_{n-1}L_{K(n)}X}{\times} L_{n-1}X
          %p
            it follows that in principle one may study a spectrum
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            by understanding all its “chromatic pieces”
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi L
                      %mrow
                        %mi K
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi n
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_{K(n)} X
